{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","statement":"React Compiler is designed to work best with React 19, but it also supports React 17 and 18.","source_span":"React Compiler is designed to work best with React 19, but it also supports React 17 and 18."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","statement":"React Compiler is installed as a devDependency via `npm install -D babel-plugin-react-compiler@latest`.","source_span":"npm install -D babel-plugin-react-compiler@latest"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","statement":"React Compiler's Babel plugin must run first in the Babel plugin pipeline because it needs the original source information for proper analysis.","source_span":"React Compiler must run **first** in your Babel plugin pipeline. The compiler needs the original source information for proper analysis, so it must process your code before other transformations."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","statement":"The reactCompilerPreset can be used with Vite only if using version 6.0.0 or later of @vitejs/plugin-react.","source_span":"If you use Vite with version 6.0.0 or later of `@vitejs/plugin-react`, you can use the `reactCompilerPreset`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","statement":"In @vitejs/plugin-react@6.0.0, the inline Babel option was removed.","source_span":"In `@vitejs/plugin-react@6.0.0`, the inline Babel option was removed."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","statement":"When the React Compiler ESLint rule reports an error, the compiler will skip optimizing that specific component or hook but will continue optimizing other parts of the codebase.","source_span":"When the ESLint rule reports an error, it means the compiler will skip optimizing that specific component or hook. This is safe: the compiler will continue optimizing other parts of your codebase."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","statement":"Components optimized by React Compiler show a \"Memo ✨\" badge in React DevTools.","source_span":"Components optimized by React Compiler will show a \"Memo ✨\" badge in React DevTools"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","statement":"React Native uses Babel via Metro, so the Babel setup instructions apply for installing React Compiler in React Native.","source_span":"React Native uses Babel via Metro, so refer to the [Usage with Babel](#babel) section for installation instructions."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Which React versions does React Compiler support, and which version does it work best with?","answer":"React Compiler is designed to work best with React 19, but it also supports React 17 and 18."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What npm command installs React Compiler as a devDependency?","answer":"npm install -D babel-plugin-react-compiler@latest"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Where must the React Compiler Babel plugin be placed in the Babel plugin pipeline, and why?","answer":"It must run first in the pipeline, because the compiler needs the original source information for proper analysis before other transformations process the code."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What version of @vitejs/plugin-react is required to use the reactCompilerPreset, and what changed in that version?","answer":"Version 6.0.0 or later of @vitejs/plugin-react is required to use reactCompilerPreset; in that version, the inline Babel option was removed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","fact_refs":[5],"question":"If the React Compiler ESLint rule reports an error on a component, does the compiler stop optimizing the rest of the codebase?","answer":"No. The compiler only skips optimizing that specific component or hook; it continues optimizing the other parts of the codebase safely."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How can you visually confirm in React DevTools that a component has been optimized by React Compiler?","answer":"Optimized components show a \"Memo ✨\" badge in React DevTools."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_installation","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How should React Native developers set up React Compiler, given that Metro is used?","answer":"Since React Native uses Babel via Metro, they should follow the standard Babel setup instructions for installing React Compiler."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","statement":"React Compiler is a new build-time tool that automatically optimizes React apps.","source_span":"React Compiler is a new build-time tool that automatically optimizes your React app."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","statement":"React Compiler works with plain JavaScript and understands the Rules of React, so developers don't need to rewrite code to use it.","source_span":"It works with plain JavaScript, and understands the [Rules of React]..., so you don't need to rewrite any code to use it."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","statement":"React Compiler's automatic memoization focuses on two use cases: skipping cascading re-rendering of components, and skipping expensive calculations from outside of React.","source_span":"it focuses on these two use cases: 1. **Skipping cascading re-rendering of components** ... 2. **Skipping expensive calculations from outside of React**"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","statement":"Without manual memoization, when a component's state changes, React re-renders that component and all of its children.","source_span":"when a component's state changes, React will re-render that component _and all of its children_"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","statement":"In the manual memoization example, even though handleClick is wrapped in useCallback, the arrow function `() => handleClick(item)` creates a new function on every render, which breaks Item's memoization.","source_span":"Even though `handleClick` is wrapped in `useCallback`, the arrow function `() => handleClick(item)` creates a new function every time the component renders. This means that `Item` will always receive a new `onClick` prop, breaking memoization."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","statement":"React Compiler only memoizes React components and hooks, not every function.","source_span":"React Compiler only memoizes React components and hooks, not every function"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is React Compiler and when does it perform its optimization?","answer":"React Compiler is a build-time tool that automatically optimizes a React app; the optimization happens at build time, not runtime."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Do developers need to rewrite their code to use React Compiler?","answer":"No. React Compiler works with plain JavaScript and understands the Rules of React, so no code rewrite is needed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What two use cases does React Compiler's automatic memoization primarily focus on?","answer":"It focuses on skipping cascading re-rendering of components and skipping expensive calculations that occur outside of React."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Without manual memoization, what happens to a component's children when that component's state changes?","answer":"React will re-render the component and all of its children."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","fact_refs":[4],"question":"In the ExpensiveComponent example, why does wrapping handleClick in useCallback fail to prevent Item from receiving a new onClick prop each render?","answer":"Because the inline arrow function `() => handleClick(item)` passed to Item creates a new function on every render, so Item always gets a new onClick prop even though handleClick itself is memoized with useCallback."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_introduction","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Does React Compiler memoize every function in a codebase, or only specific ones?","answer":"React Compiler only memoizes React components and hooks, not every function."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","statement":"React lets developers describe the different states a component can be in and switch between them in response to user input, instead of directly manipulating the UI.","source_span":"you describe the different states that your component can be in, and switch between them in response to the user input"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","statement":"Thinking about UI declaratively involves five steps: identify visual states, determine what triggers state changes, represent state with useState, remove non-essential state variables, and connect event handlers to set the state.","source_span":"1. **Identify** your component's different visual states\n2. **Determine** what triggers those state changes\n3. **Represent** the state in memory using `useState`\n4. **Remove** any non-essential state variables\n5. **Connect** the event handlers to set the state"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","statement":"The example quiz form component has five visual states: Empty, Typing, Submitting, Success, and Error.","source_span":"**Empty**: Form has a disabled \"Submit\" button.\n* **Typing**: Form has an enabled \"Submit\" button.\n* **Submitting**: Form is completely disabled. Spinner is shown.\n* **Success**: \"Thank you\" message is shown instead of a form.\n* **Error**: Same as Typing state, but with an extra error message."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","statement":"In the imperative example's submitForm function, the answer only resolves as correct if it equals \"istanbul\" (case-insensitive).","source_span":"if (answer.toLowerCase() === 'istanbul') {\n        resolve();"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","statement":"The imperative example's submitForm function simulates a network request using a 1500ms setTimeout before resolving or rejecting.","source_span":"setTimeout(() => {\n      if (answer.toLowerCase() === 'istanbul') {\n        resolve();\n      } else {\n        reject(new Error('Good guess but a wrong answer. Try again!'));\n      }\n    }, 1500);"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","statement":"The document compares imperative UI programming to riding in a car and giving turn-by-turn directions, and compares declarative UI programming (React) to telling a taxi driver the destination.","source_span":"imagine riding next to someone in a car and telling them turn by turn where to go... Think of getting into a taxi and telling the driver where you want to go instead of telling them exactly where to turn."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","fact_refs":[0],"question":"According to the document, how does React let you handle UI changes in response to user input, as opposed to manipulating the UI directly?","answer":"React lets you describe the different states your component can be in and switch between them in response to user input, rather than manipulating individual pieces of the UI directly."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What five steps does the document recommend for thinking about UI declaratively in React?","answer":"Identify the component's different visual states, determine what triggers those state changes, represent the state in memory using useState, remove any non-essential state variables, and connect the event handlers to set the state."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the five visual states of the example quiz form component?","answer":"Empty (disabled Submit button), Typing (enabled Submit button), Submitting (form disabled, spinner shown), Success (\"Thank you\" message shown), and Error (same as Typing but with an extra error message)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","fact_refs":[3],"question":"In the city quiz example's submitForm function, what answer causes the promise to resolve successfully?","answer":"The promise resolves successfully only if the answer, lowercased, equals \"istanbul\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How long does the simulated network request take in the imperative example's submitForm function before it resolves or rejects?","answer":"It takes 1500 milliseconds (simulated via setTimeout) before resolving or rejecting."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_reacting-to-input-with-state","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What driving analogy does the document use to contrast imperative UI programming with declarative UI programming?","answer":"Imperative programming is compared to riding in a car and giving turn-by-turn directions, while declarative programming (React) is compared to telling a taxi driver the destination and letting them figure out how to get there."}
